libdrm on illumos
=================

This directory builds libdrm 2.4.134 (https://dri.freedesktop.org/libdrm/,
SHA-256 ac5e74d157830eb8bee44c6a6bf3ad49774ef0dd2a72bdad74a8f20308b52a95)
with the patches in patches/ and the illumos sources in
usr/src/lib/libdrm/common.  It serves two kernels:

 - gfx-drm's own drm and i915 modules (usr/src/uts), Intel gen2 to gen7.5
   at the level of Linux 3.12;
 - newer DRM kernel drivers that provide card and render nodes.


Version
-------

2.4.134 was the current release on 2026-09-30.  Mesa needs:

	Mesa		libdrm	libdrm_amdgpu	libdrm_intel
	25.0 - 25.2	2.4.109	2.4.121		2.4.75
	26.2, main	2.4.109	2.4.133		2.4.75

crocus, iris, virgl and venus need only libdrm itself (2.4.109; they carry
their own uAPI headers).  libdrm_amdgpu 2.4.133 is the highest requirement
(radeonsi, radv), and 2.4.134 meets it.


Patches (patches/)
------------------

Kept, rebased:
  incl-drm-drm-h.patch       drm_handle_t is 64 bits wide, the drm_map,
                             drm_buf_desc and drm_buf_map layouts of the
                             gfx-drm kernel, _DRM_GEM, and the illumos
                             _IOC() (see "ioctl numbers").  The patched
                             header must match uts/common/drm/drm.h.
  incl-drm-i915-drm-h.patch  sarea padding, IOW instead of IOWR for the
                             three legacy ioctls that copy out themselves,
                             and the 32-bit argument structures of the
                             gfx-drm i915 kernel.
  intel-drm.patch            drm_intel_bufmgr_gem_find() also matches a
                             different descriptor of the same open file;
                             a gcc warning in intel_decode.c.
  exynos.patch               gcc -Wmaybe-uninitialized in exynos_fimg2d.c
                             (only the warning fix is left).
  modesetting.patch          drmCheckModesettingSupported() returns 0.
  pkgconfig-in.patch         -R${libdir} and the include/drm directory.

Rewritten:
  xf86drm-c.patch            the illumos branches of xf86drm.c, which call
                             into lib/libdrm/common/sun_devinfo.c and
                             sun_ioctl.c (below).

Dropped (upstream no longer needs them):
  etnaviv.patch, omap.patch, freedreno.patch  (no <linux/*.h> includes
                             left upstream), libdrm-lists-h.patch,
                             util-double-list-h.patch (upstream uses
                             __typeof__), xf86drm-h.patch (the _sun_drm_*
                             helpers are no longer public; their prototypes
                             are in the private sun_devinfo.h).

Upstream removed libkms (2.4.111) and tests/kms, tests/kmstest; the gate
no longer builds or packages them.  No OpenIndiana component links libkms.

generated_static_table_fourcc.h is generated at build time from the
patched drm_fourcc.h by upstream's gen_table_fourcc.py (Makefile, target
.patched), as meson does.


The device layer (lib/libdrm/common/sun_devinfo.c)
--------------------------------------------------

Linux libdrm answers "which node is this file, which device is it, which
bus" from sysfs.  On illumos:

 - Nodes are the /dev/dri links made by devfsadm's SUNW_drm_link for minor
   nodes of type ddi_display:drm.  The node type comes from the link name
   (card<N> primary, renderD<N> render, controlD<N> control), not from the
   minor number:

	kernel		minor name	link		node minor
	newer drivers	card<N>		card<N>		N
			renderD<128+N>	renderD<128+N>	128 + N
	gfx-drm		drm<N>		card<N>		N
			controlD<N>	controlD<N>	64 + N

 - Every open is a clone open in both kernels: the file's minor number has
   the node minor in its low nine bits and a clone id above.  A file is
   matched to its node by the major number and the low nine bits, so every
   open of a node (not only the first) finds it.  Majors are dynamic and
   per driver; there is no DRM_MAJOR and libdrm never creates nodes.
 - Links that resolve to the same /devices path (up to the ':') belong to
   the same device; that pairs a card node with its render node.
 - The device node is read with libdevinfo.  A node counts as DRM only if
   its device has a minor of type ddi_display:drm with that dev_t,
   whatever the device node is called (VGA functions are "display",
   virtio-gpu is "pci1af4,1100").  If the parent nexus has device_type
   "pci" or "pciex", the device is DRM_BUS_PCI: bus, device and function
   from its "reg" (or "assigned-addresses") property, vendor, device,
   subsystem and revision from its PCI properties; the PCI domain is 0.  A
   child of the pseudo nexus (a driver without hardware) is DRM_BUS_FAUX,
   named after its devfs node ("<driver>@0"), as Linux reports vgem and
   vkms.  Other buses are not enumerated.

xf86drm.c uses this for drmGetDevices2, drmGetDevice2 (and
drmGetDeviceFromDevId), drmGetNodeTypeFromFd, drmGetDeviceNameFromFd,
drmGetDeviceNameFromFd2, drmGetPrimaryDeviceNameFromFd,
drmGetRenderDeviceNameFromFd and the minor-type lookup behind them.
/opt/drm-tests/amd64/getsundev checks all of them for every node.


ioctl numbers
-------------

DRM_IO*() in drm.h are the illumos _IO*() of <sys/ioccom.h>: direction in
the top bits, the argument size masked to eight bits (IOCPARM_MASK) at
bit 16, 'd' at bit 8, the number in the low byte.  A kernel therefore
cannot learn a size above 255 bytes from the command.  drm.h's own _IOC(),
which drmCommandRead/Write/WriteRead() use through DRM_IOC(), masks the
size the same way, so both paths give the same command for the same
argument (before 2.4.134 it did not, and sizes above 255 bytes differed).

Four core structures grew since libdrm 2.4.109, so their commands have new
numbers: drm_syncobj_handle (16 -> 24 bytes, SYNCOBJ_HANDLE_TO_FD and
SYNCOBJ_FD_TO_HANDLE), drm_syncobj_wait (32 -> 40, SYNCOBJ_WAIT) and
drm_syncobj_timeline_wait (40 -> 48, SYNCOBJ_TIMELINE_WAIT).  Linux's
drm_ioctl() accepts every size; a kernel that matches the whole command
value and knows only the 2.4.109 sizes answers ENOTTY.  drmIoctl() therefore
retries such a command with the older size when the kernel answers ENOTTY
and the added fields are zero (sun_ioctl.c).  The virtgpu commands
EXECBUFFER (32/40/64 bytes) and TRANSFER_* (36/44) grew as well; a
virtio-gpu driver has to accept all their sizes itself.
No drm_mode.h or i915_drm.h structure changed size.


Headers
-------

drm.h, drm_fourcc.h, drm_mode.h, drm_sarea.h and i915_drm.h are shipped
from uts/common/drm (package system/header/header-drm) and must be
identical to the patched libdrm copies; "make check" in this directory
compares them.  The other uAPI headers (amdgpu, mach64, mga, msm, nouveau,
qxl, r128, radeon, savage, sis, tegra, vc4, via, virtgpu, vmwgfx) are
shipped unchanged from libdrm by x11/library/libdrm.  The headers define
more than either kernel implements; a constant in a header does not mean
the running kernel supports it.  Ask the kernel (DRM_IOCTL_GET_CAP,
DRM_IOCTL_VERSION, driver GETPARAMs) instead.

What the gfx-drm kernel implements:

  gfx-drm drm + i915 (usr/src/uts):
    core      VERSION, GET_UNIQUE, GET_MAGIC, IRQ_BUSID, GET_MAP,
              GET_CLIENT, GET_STATS, GET_CAP (DUMB_BUFFER,
              VBLANK_HIGH_CRTC, DUMB_PREFERRED_DEPTH, DUMB_PREFER_SHADOW),
              SET_VERSION, SET_UNIQUE, AUTH_MAGIC, SET_MASTER,
              DROP_MASTER, the DRI1 map, context, lock, buffer, AGP and
              scatter/gather ioctls, WAIT_VBLANK, MODESET_CTL
    GEM       GEM_CLOSE, GEM_FLINK, GEM_OPEN, MODE_CREATE_DUMB,
              MODE_MAP_DUMB, MODE_DESTROY_DUMB
    KMS       MODE_GETRESOURCES, MODE_GETPLANERESOURCES, MODE_GETCRTC,
              MODE_SETCRTC, MODE_GETPLANE, MODE_SETPLANE, MODE_CURSOR,
              MODE_CURSOR2, MODE_GETGAMMA, MODE_SETGAMMA, MODE_GETENCODER,
              MODE_GETCONNECTOR, MODE_GETPROPERTY, MODE_SETPROPERTY,
              MODE_GETPROPBLOB, MODE_GETFB, MODE_ADDFB, MODE_ADDFB2,
              MODE_RMFB, MODE_PAGE_FLIP, MODE_DIRTYFB,
              MODE_OBJ_GETPROPERTIES, MODE_OBJ_SETPROPERTY
    i915      INIT, FLUSH, FLIP, BATCHBUFFER, IRQ_EMIT, IRQ_WAIT,
              GETPARAM, SETPARAM, ALLOC, FREE, INIT_HEAP, CMDBUFFER,
              DESTROY_HEAP, GET_VBLANK_PIPE, VBLANK_SWAP, HWS_ADDR,
              GEM_INIT, GEM_EXECBUFFER,
              GEM_EXECBUFFER2, GEM_PIN, GEM_UNPIN, GEM_BUSY,
              GEM_SET_CACHING, GEM_GET_CACHING, GEM_THROTTLE,
              GEM_ENTERVT, GEM_LEAVEVT, GEM_CREATE, GEM_PREAD, GEM_PWRITE,
              GEM_MMAP, GEM_MMAP_GTT, GEM_SET_DOMAIN, GEM_SW_FINISH,
              GEM_SET_TILING, GEM_GET_TILING, GEM_GET_APERTURE,
              GET_PIPE_FROM_CRTC_ID, GEM_MADVISE, OVERLAY_PUT_IMAGE,
              OVERLAY_ATTRS, SET_SPRITE_COLORKEY, GET_SPRITE_COLORKEY,
              GEM_WAIT, GEM_CONTEXT_CREATE, GEM_CONTEXT_DESTROY, REG_READ
    Not: render nodes, PRIME, sync objects, SET_CLIENT_CAP, atomic,
    GETFB2, the i915 ioctls after Linux 3.12.
